## 5. Phase 4 — Package contracts and exports

Specify future package metadata and public API requirements without creating an implementation package in this task.

### Audit and design

For every proposed published package, define:

- package ID/name proposal and purpose;
- public import paths and supported subpath exports;
- ESM/CJS policy if either is supported; do not promise both without consumer/build evidence;
- browser and server entrypoints if needed;
- `types`/declaration file strategy;
- package `exports`, `files`, `sideEffects`, `peerDependencies`, `dependencies`, `optionalDependencies`, `engines`, `type`, `license`, `repository`, `publishConfig` and metadata policy;
- CSS and token entrypoints;
- whether consumers import generated CSS explicitly;
- whether package requires Tailwind plugin/config or only CSS imports;
- how a consumer uses the library in Vite, Next.js/SSR, or other supported setups;
- which imports are stable public API vs internal/private;
- how an unsupported deep import should fail;
- dependency duplication and React peer dependency strategy;
- minimum supported React/TypeScript/Node/browser ranges, derived from audit/research;
- package size/bundle budget and optional-module loading;
- source map strategy and whether source files are shipped;
- tarball contents and inspection plan;
- license, NOTICE/attribution and provenance policy.

Do not set version numbers or engines before the audit. Do not invent the npm scope or use placeholders that appear final. Use a conspicuous `proposed` state for unresolved fields.

### API stability

Define rules for:

- public exports vs internal files;
- public props/types/utilities;
- tokens/CSS custom properties;
- skin IDs and contract schema;
- component/recipe names;
- registry item names and dependencies;
- deprecations, migration warnings and removal timelines;
- breaking vs additive changes;
- how source-registry consumers get changed files and upgrade notes;
- how generated files differ from canonical source.

Any rename/removal of a stable skin ID or public token/API requires change classification and migration planning. Avoid silently breaking imports.

