## 6. Phase 5 — Build, CSS, Tailwind, and token pipeline integration

Research how Moonframe can deliver its themes reliably with Tailwind CSS v4 and CSS custom properties without forcing consumers into brittle configuration.

Specify:

1. canonical token source and schema location;
2. token validation and type/alias rules;
3. deterministic generation process;
4. generated output path and whether committed or generated during build;
5. CSS entrypoints for baseline styles, tokens, skin recipes and optional effects;
6. `@theme` usage versus runtime CSS custom properties and when each is appropriate;
7. how 15 skins and Light/Dark maps are selected at runtime;
8. how consumers import token CSS and which styles are intentionally not auto-imported;
9. compatibility with consumer Tailwind scanning/content detection and static utility discovery;
10. prevention of unbounded dynamically constructed class names;
11. CSS layer order and reset/preflight ownership;
12. font loading and fallback strategy, including licensed-font policy;
13. CSS isolation/namespacing and consumer override mechanisms;
14. SSR/hydration and avoidance of flash-of-wrong-theme;
15. no-JS/failed-asset graceful behavior;
16. reduced-motion/forced-colors and consumer override handling;
17. CSS size and selector/variable budget;
18. deterministic outputs, source maps, and validation of stale generated files.

Do not finalize all token values or produce theme assets here. This is package/DX architecture; refer to the Token Architecture & Theme Engine Bible and ensure this document adds only distribution/build/consumer integration aspects rather than duplicating token semantics.

---

## 7. Phase 6 — Consumer developer journey (DX) specification

Design the entire experience of installing, using, customizing, debugging, upgrading, and contributing to Moonframe.

### Consumer journeys to document

- Install the core package in a fresh supported app.
- Import Button/Input and its styles/types.
- Setup theme/token CSS with Tailwind v4.
- Set a skin and Light/Dark/System mode.
- Override a token safely.
- Use a premium block with sample props/data.
- Add a registry block/template into a consumer-owned project, if this distribution model is approved.
- Add an optional adapter without pulling a large dependency into core.
- Use the library in supported SSR/hydration context.
- Diagnose missing CSS, missing skin tokens, unresolved exports, dependency mismatch, hydration mismatch, or duplicate React.
- Upgrade versions and understand breaking changes.
- Contribute a component/skin/recipe and run the relevant validation locally.

### Required DX artifacts

Specify which guides need to exist and their acceptance criteria:

- Quick start (shortest verified path from blank app to rendered component).
- Installation by supported framework/bundler.
- Tailwind v4/theme CSS setup.
- Theme/skin configuration and persistence.
- Import and tree-shaking guide.
- Customization and extension guide.
- Premium blocks/registry guide, if relevant.
- Optional integrations guide.
- SSR/hydration guide if relevant.
- Troubleshooting/FAQ.
- Upgrade/migration guide.
- Component/API docs and live examples.
- Contribution guide and test commands.
- Architecture overview and ADR index.
- Accessibility and skin QA guide.
- Release/support policy.

Every guide must be derived from a verified implementation later; at this stage, define the planned documentation contract and label examples as proposed/specification. Do not fabricate tested commands or claim that a quick start works before it is executed.

### Developer errors and diagnostics

Research and specify useful diagnostics for common failures:

- token stylesheet missing;
- unknown skin ID;
- unresolved semantic token;
- unsupported mode/density value;
- multiple React versions;
- missing peer dependency;
- unsupported import path;
- stale generated token outputs;
- broken optional adapter import;
- mismatched Tailwind version/config;
- registry schema/version mismatch;
- SSR theme mismatch.

Define when each should be a build-time error, development warning, runtime fallback, or documentation-only warning. Avoid noisy runtime logs in production.

