## 12. Phase 11 — CI quality gates and consumer validation

Specify a minimal-but-strong CI system based on actual repo tools. Do not create an overengineered matrix without an evidence-backed need.

### Documentation/contract gates now

- JSON and schema validation for current contracts.
- Exactly 15 stable skin IDs; 30 baseline skin/mode combinations.
- Exactly 105 unique unordered skin-pair entries in the grammar contract.
- Links/paths resolve; references match existing canonical files.
- No duplicate/contradictory package names and status labels.
- Architecture ADR and research log contain required fields.
- Roadmap tasks reference current docs and do not claim implementation prematurely.

### Future package gates (specify only)

- lint, typecheck, unit/integration tests;
- API/export snapshot or public API review;
- token generator determinism and stale-output checks;
- build all supported package entrypoints;
- React/peer dependency checks;
- `npm pack`/package contents check;
- install the tarball into clean Vite and any genuinely supported SSR consumer fixture;
- verify declarations resolve under supported TypeScript versions;
- confirm CSS/token entrypoints work in Tailwind v4;
- confirm tree-shaking/optional modules and dependency isolation;
- smoke test docs examples against actual versioned exports;
- accessibility/visual regression plans tied to the other canonical QA document;
- dependency/security/license/provenance scans where justified.

Do not implement those future gates as product code now unless a small documentation-only validator is necessary and safely scoped. State exactly which validations were executed and which merely proposed.

