## 13. Phase 12 — Governance and contribution experience

Define the future contributor workflow for maintainers and AI coding agents:

1. read the master Bible and relevant contracts;
2. locate canonical source of truth for the change;
3. create/choose a small issue with acceptance criteria;
4. make scoped changes and document rationale;
5. run relevant validation commands;
6. update docs/contracts/examples in the same change where required;
7. provide screenshots and accessibility evidence for visual changes;
8. add migration note for breaking public APIs/tokens/skin IDs;
9. request review and meet release gates.

Define statuses such as `proposed`, `specified`, `implemented`, `tested`, `reviewed`, `released`. Do not compress them into a single checkbox.

Specify a template for architecture decision records (ADRs), component/package change proposals, release checklists, issue templates, and pull request templates only if these templates are missing and appropriate for the repo. Avoid creating duplicate templates that conflict with existing conventions.

AI agents must not invent package names, add dependencies without justification, rename stable IDs, bypass tests, or publish releases. They must report actual evidence and unimplemented work honestly.

