## 16. Roadmap requirements

Build a phased task roadmap small enough for developer/AI agents to execute without making broad architecture decisions. Each task must include:

- stable task ID;
- objective;
- scope and non-goals;
- prerequisites/dependencies;
- likely paths affected;
- acceptance criteria;
- validation commands or planned validation (do not invent commands before toolchain audit);
- docs/contracts to update;
- accessibility/security/packaging concerns if relevant;
- risk, rollback and migration impact;
- definition of done and honest status.

Suggested phases:

1. repository audit and research;
2. decide package topology and distribution model;
3. define public import/export and package metadata contracts;
4. define CSS/Tailwind/token integration;
5. define consumer quickstart and supported frameworks;
6. define documentation site/Skin Lab DX;
7. define registry/CLI scope or explicit deferment;
8. define package build/type declarations/consumer validation;
9. define CI, release/versioning and supply-chain policy;
10. reconcile all Bibles/contracts and prepare issue-ready backlog;
11. implement only after a separately approved implementation phase.

Do not create hundreds of GitHub issues at once. Audit and deduplicate first. If issue tooling is unavailable, provide issue-ready backlog Markdown or JSON. Claim issues were created only if each creation is verified.

---

## 17. Quality bar and anti-patterns

Reject the following:

- splitting each token family, skin, icon set, or block category into its own npm package without demonstrated need;
- packaging all premium blocks and all optional engines into the core bundle;
- claiming “tree-shakeable” without package metadata and consumer-bundle evidence later;
- relying only on workspace symlinks and never testing packed tarballs;
- shipping CSS that consumers cannot override safely;
- mixing Tailwind v3 assumptions with v4 without explicit support policy;
- dynamic class-name construction that Tailwind cannot detect;
- forcing consumer applications into a proprietary build pipeline for basic usage;
- duplicating docs/source-of-truth across multiple Bibles;
- confusing a component npm package with a source-owning registry;
- publishing a registry/CLI before validating the install/upgrade use case;
- selecting tools because they are fashionable or because popular templates use them;
- claiming current Node/npm requirements without verifying official docs and consumer constraints;
- storing long-lived publish tokens when a supported trusted-publishing flow is appropriate;
- configuring OIDC/provenance in documentation as if it is already enabled;
- claiming package readiness without clean-install/tarball/consumer tests;
- implementing UI components, auth/backend behavior, or production art/SFX during this documentation-only task;
- marking missing test results as passing;
- claiming all 15 skins are distinct without evidence from Skin Lab and pairwise reviews.

---

## 18. Definition of done for this task

This task is complete only when:

1. The actual GitHub repository and starting commit are verified.
2. All relevant current files, issues/PRs and existing decisions have been audited.
3. An audit report exists with evidence-based findings and severity.
4. Official current documentation has been researched and logged, with source dates/version status where available.
5. Package topology and distribution options have a researched comparison; final decision or open decision is documented honestly.
6. Package metadata/export/CSS/TypeScript/peer-dependency and consumer compatibility requirements are specified.
7. Tailwind v4 and token distribution requirements link clearly to the Token Architecture & Theme Engine Bible instead of duplicating it.
8. DX journeys, docs, playground, and consumer validation have a coherent plan.
9. npm/source registry/hybrid decision includes scope, trade-offs, security, upgrade and maintenance burden.
10. Versioning, releases, trusted publishing/provenance, CI security, migration and rollback policies are specified at a level suitable for future implementation.
11. Machine-readable contracts validate if a schema/validator is included.
12. All links/paths/filenames and README navigation are checked.
13. Existing documents have been refined rather than duplicated, or a documented reason explains the new file.
14. A roadmap and issue-ready tasks have dependencies and acceptance criteria.
15. Actual writes/commits are verified remotely if made; no branch push/commit is claimed without evidence.
16. The final report separates what was completed, what was validated, what is specified only, open decisions, and out-of-scope implementation.

---

## 19. Required final report to the user (Bahasa Indonesia)

Return a specific report containing:

- repository URL, branch, starting and final commit SHAs;
- P0/P1/P2/P3 audit findings with evidence;
- sources researched and key decisions they informed;
- current package topology recommendation and alternatives rejected/deferred;
- distribution recommendation: npm, registry, hybrid or deferment;
- list of files added/changed and why;
- machine-readable checks run and their actual results;
- tests/validators not run and why;
- whether issue backlog items were created or only documented;
- explicitly unimplemented items (runtime package, components, assets, registry, CLI, publishing, SFX);
- open decisions, risks and dependencies;
- recommended next 3–5 tasks in dependency order.

Never claim “production-ready”, “published”, “verified consumer installation”, “trusted publishing enabled”, “all package exports work”, or “DX is proven” without direct evidence.

---

## 20. Start now

Begin with **Phase 0 — audit the actual repository**. Do not write new architecture docs until you know what exists. Then research official current sources, create the audit report, compare package/distribution options, refine the canonical architecture/DX documentation and machine-readable contracts, validate links/schema, and verify repository writes.

The outcome must be a clear, evidence-based **Package Architecture & Developer Experience Bible** that tells future developers exactly how Moonframe should be organized, consumed, customized, documented, tested, versioned, distributed and maintained—without prematurely forcing a technology or implementing the UI before the specification is ready.
